WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:01.980
- [Narrator] At least two federal agencies

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:03.900
are investigating Silicon Valley Bank

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:05.340
after it collapsed in March.

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:08.820
According to exclusive reporting
by the Wall Street Journal

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:11.490
Dave Michaels, a reporter
covering white collar crime

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:13.350
and financial enforcement at the Journal

00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:15.364
breaks down what
investigators are probing.

00:00:15.364 --> 00:00:17.720
(gentle suspenseful music)

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:20.400
- SVB fell victim to a bank run.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.950
It was a bank run and
sort of the classic sense

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:26.580
where depositors all
sought their money back

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:30.390
at the same time, 42
billion worth of deposits

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:32.400
slow down of the bank on one date.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:34.530
It's very common for
the Justice Department

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:38.940
and the Securities and Exchange
Commission to investigate

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:42.240
after a big public company or a bank fails

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:45.150
or any other kind of very
negative market event.

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:47.970
SVB Financial Group is a public company

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:49.710
who's shares are traded publicly

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:52.620
and it owned Silicon Valley Bank.

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:57.300
The SEC is likely to examine
what the public company said

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:00.870
about the risks to Silicon Valley Bank.

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:05.870
Did the public company disclose
all the facts and risks

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:08.190
that shareholders needed

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:11.580
in order to make informed
investment decisions?

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:14.910
The Justice Department can
look at a broader set of facts.

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:16.830
Their jurisdiction is not just limited

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:19.170
to capital markets or public companies

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:23.550
so it's likely that the DOJ
could be looking at matters

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:25.380
that are a little bit broader

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:28.860
than just how the, how
the public company worked.

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:33.860
Technically, DOJ and SEC
investigations are separate

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:39.110
but it is not unusual that
the two agencies cooperate.

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:43.110
In the end they make, you
know, separate decisions

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:46.020
sometimes on different timetables

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:49.680
about whether to bring
charges or civil claims.

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:51.930
The two agencies are
also, as we understand it,

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:54.840
looking at trades, stock sales

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:58.980
that the CEO and CFO of
the public company made

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:01.080
in close proximity to the bank's failure.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:04.560
So about three weeks
before the bank failed

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:07.650
the CEO and the CFO sold in total

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:12.303
what amounts to about 2.7 or
$2.8 million worth of stock.

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:17.940
Anytime you see stock
sales in close proximity

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:21.390
to a very negative event,
it raises the suspicions

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:24.450
of a lot of people, including
regulators in law enforcement

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:27.870
and they are going to
look at the stock sales,

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:31.230
try to find out if the
executives had material,

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:34.620
non-public information, that
wasn't known to the market.

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:37.620
And if they had that, if they
were in possession of that,

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:39.420
then they weren't supposed to trade.

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:43.470
The Justice Department is conducting

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:45.810
as far as we know, a
criminal investigation.

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:48.240
The SEC is a civil regulatory agency

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:50.880
so its authority is limited

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:54.510
to fining companies,
civil penalties that is.

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:57.720
There isn't a timeframe that the SEC

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:00.240
or the DOJ have to stick to.

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:01.920
There is gonna be a period here

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:04.050
where the SEC is doing some investigations

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:08.700
whether they lead to any
lawsuits or claims of wrongdoing?

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:10.770
It's impossible to know at this point

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:12.546
and we might not know for a while.

00:03:12.546 --> 00:03:15.546
(melancholic music)

